From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] diag: temporary eprintln! logging around the riscv64
 empty-tuple bug

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [temporary riscv64 CI diagnostic, to be reverted once read]

Every riscv64 CI run of this port shows the identical "83 failed, 35
passed, 1 skipped" with the dominant symptom being wsgi.py's
`body_resp, status_code, headers_map = result` unpacking a genuinely
empty tuple from handle_request - confirmed NOT caused by the mimalloc
allocator, and NOT caused by the release/LTO/opt-level profile (a full
debug build with no optimization at all reproduces the exact same
result). Source reading narrowed this to the "full pipeline" path
(session/make_response/_response_to_rust_format), which for a session
test calls into this crate's Rust-backed Signer::sign for cookie
signing - a separate failure cluster ("quote_from_bytes() expected
bytes") plausibly shares this same root cause if sign() is not handing
back a clean value on riscv64.

Adds eprintln! (not tracing::error!, since the test client calls
handle_request directly without ever starting the server or
initializing tracing_subscriber) at two points: right where
Signer::sign returns (input/output lengths and a prefix of the signed
value), and in handle_request both right after process_request returns
and right before the final tuple is constructed (status/body/headers
lengths at each point). This is diagnostic only - to be reverted once
the next real CI log is read, never meant to ship.
---
 src/bindings/app.rs | 19 +++++++++++++++++--
 src/crypto.rs       | 11 ++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 27 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/bindings/app.rs b/src/bindings/app.rs
index d95843c..dced8b2 100644
--- a/src/bindings/app.rs
+++ b/src/bindings/app.rs
@@ -413,14 +413,29 @@ impl PyBustApp {
         let routes = state.routes.load_full();
         let response_data = routes.process_request(req_data);

+        eprintln!(
+            "RISCV64_DIAG handle_request: path={:?} status={} body_len={} headers_count={}",
+            path,
+            response_data.status.as_u16(),
+            response_data.body.len(),
+            response_data.headers.len()
+        );
+
         let body_str = response_data
             .body_as_string()
             .unwrap_or_else(|_| String::from_utf8_lossy(&response_data.body).to_string());

-        Ok((
+        let out = (
             body_str,
             response_data.status.as_u16(),
             response_data.headers,
-        ))
+        );
+        eprintln!(
+            "RISCV64_DIAG handle_request: returning tuple body_str_len={} status={} headers_vec_len={}",
+            out.0.len(),
+            out.1,
+            out.2.len()
+        );
+        Ok(out)
     }
 }
diff --git a/src/crypto.rs b/src/crypto.rs
index b4c4bf9..b47e6ab 100644
--- a/src/crypto.rs
+++ b/src/crypto.rs
@@ -42,8 +42,17 @@ impl Signer {
         jar.signed_mut(&self.key).add(c);

         if let Some(cookie) = jar.get(name) {
-            Ok(cookie.value().to_string())
+            let signed = cookie.value().to_string();
+            eprintln!(
+                "RISCV64_DIAG sign: name={:?} in_len={} out_len={} out_prefix={:?}",
+                name,
+                value.len(),
+                signed.len(),
+                &signed[..signed.len().min(24)]
+            );
+            Ok(signed)
         } else {
+            eprintln!("RISCV64_DIAG sign: name={:?} FAILED jar.get returned None", name);
             Err(pyo3::exceptions::PyValueError::new_err(
                 "Failed to sign cookie",
             ))
--
